air, leftover W3, W3, W8 \u2192 need another meld? Could use two W3 as pair? But then two pairs? Actually, 14 tiles: after draw, hand becomes 5 tiles: W2, W2, W3, W3, W8. Plus melds: 9 tiles. To win, need 4 melds+pair. Currently melds: B3-B5 chow, W4 pung, F4 pung = 3 melds. Need one more meld from hand. My hand could make pung of W3? But only two W3, not pung. Could make pair of W3 and pair of W2, but that's two pairs, which doesn't fit 4 melds+1 pair pattern. So not winning. So discarding W3 seems to leave no clear wait.  \n\nThus, discarding W8 or W2 seem better. Between them, discarding W8 leaves wait for W4 or W2. Discarding W2 leaves wait only for W8. Number of winning tiles:  \n- Discard W8: 1 W4 + 1 W2 = 2 tiles.  \n- Discard W2: 1 W8 = 1 tile.  \nSo discarding W8 gives more chances.  \n\nAlso consider safety: W8 is relatively safe? Looking at discards: previous player played W8 once. Next player played W8 once. Across played none. W8 unshown:1, so one left. W2: unshown 1, discards: previous played W2 once, I played W2 once? My history: \"Play W2\" after Peng W4. So W2 is somewhat dangerous. But both are terminal-ish? W8 is not terminal (terminal is 1 or 9). W2 is simple.  \n\nGiven I need to maximize winning chances, discard W8 is slightly better.  \n\nAlso consider potential scoring: If I win with chow W2-W3-W4, my hand would have: chow B3-B5, chow W2-W3-W4, pung W4, pung F4, and pair from draw? Actually, wait: after discard W8, I have W2 and three W3. Draw W4: then I can form chow W2-W3-W4 using one W2, one W3, one W4. Leftover: two W3. So pair is W3. Melds: B3-B5 (chow), W2-W3-W4 (chow), W4 pung (exposed), F4 pung (exposed). That's 4 melds and pair W3. Check fan:  \n- All Chows? No, two pungs.  \n- All Simples? No, F4 is honor.  \n- Pung of Terminals or Honors: F4 pung gives 1 fan.  \n- No other obvious fan. Total maybe 1 fan, which is below 8 fan required to win. So I cannot win with that hand because it's less than 8 fan. I must have at least 8 fan to 