ybe safer? Actually, once a chow is exposed, they are less likely to need those tiles for pung. But they could still need for pair.  \n\nGiven the low fan potential, I might just discard W8 as it offers more waiting tiles, albeit still low fan. But I need to reconsider: My hand after discarding W8: W2, W3x3. If I draw W4, I can form chow W2-W3-W4, pair W3. That's only 1 fan (Pung of Honors). Not enough. If I draw W2, then I have W2 pair, and three W3 as pung. That would be melds: pung W3, pung W4, pung F4, and chow B3-B5? Wait, that's only 3 pungs and 1 chow, not 4 pungs. So not \"All Pungs\". Fan: Pung of Honors (1) + maybe \"Concealed Pung\" for W3 (if concealed, 2 fan for Two Concealed Pungs? I have no concealed pungs yet, W3 would be one concealed pung, so \"Two Concealed Pungs\" requires two concealed pungs, I only have one. So \"Concealed Pung\" gives 1 fan? Actually, patterns: \"Concealed Pung\" is not listed as a separate fan; \"Two Concealed Pungs\" is 2 fan. So one concealed pung gives 0 fan. So still only 1 fan.  \n\nThus, my hand seems hopeless to reach 8 fan unless I get a very lucky draw like another F4 to make kong? But F4 unshown:0. Or draw F2 for pung of prevailing wind (2 fan each for prevalent and seat wind? Actually \"Prevalent Wind\" and \"Seat Wind\" are separate, both 2 fan each if pung. Since both are South, a pung of F2 would give 4 fan (2+2). That could help. But I have no F2. Unshown F2:2 left. But I'd need three F2. Very unlikely.  \n\nGiven the situation, I should probably play defensively. Among W2, W3, W8, which is safest? W3: I have three, so the fourth W3 is the only one left. If someone needs W3 for pung, they might be waiting. But previous player has chow W1-W2-W3, so they might need W3 for pair? Possibly. W2: similar. W8: next player has chow W7-W8-W9, so W8 is used, maybe safe.  \n\nI'll choose to discard W8 as it seems least needed by others based on their melds.  \n\nReason: My current hand has low fan potential, unlikely to re